    "report": "Phone numbers written with hyphens are now recognised as contact details. The change is committed as `8cd15d3` on `main` and not pushed.\n\n**What changed**\n- The phone pattern is now `\\+?\\d(?:[ .-]?\\d){8,}`, so a hyphen joins two digits as a space or dot does. It is in two places, one per context that holds it, as ADR 0063 requires:\n  - Messaging, `ContactDetails.ts`: held back as `[hidden]`.\n  - Reviews, `ReviewRules.ts`: refused as `CONTACT_DETAILS`.\n- Tests added: `06-12-34-56-78` and `+33-6-12-34-56-78` are caught in both contexts. `06--12-34-56-78` (a double hyphen) is not caught.\n- `2026-12-01` is still not a phone number: it has eight digits, one short of nine.\n\n**Checks**\n- Changed specs: 22 pass.\n- Unit suite: 155 suites, 1052 tests pass.\n- E2E suites for review-rules and Messaging: 114 suites, 607 tests pass.\n- Typecheck, ESLint, Prettier on the changed files, and `yarn check:conventions` all pass.\n\n**Choices and unclear points**\n- I committed on `main` because you asked for the current branch. The git guidance says to branch first from the default branch, so tell me if you want a branch instead.\n- The ADRs describe phone numbers only in words, so I left them unchanged.\n- Existing behaviour I did not change: two dates separated by a single space or hyphen, such as `2026-12-01 2026-12-04`, count as one 16-digit run and are caught. That was already true for spaces and dots, and your rule as written includes hyphens the same way.\n- I did not start a server, so port 4471 was not used.",
